<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § test/ — "the shared provider suite (parameterised, referenced by providers)".

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES NO PROVIDER, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT SHARED. Everything a provider has to say
      about itself arrives as a ProviderConformanceCase, so adding the twentieth provider is a case
      object and two class declarations in that provider's own .Conformance project — never an edit
      here. The reference provider in Reference/ exists so this project has something to run against
      and so the suite can be shown to REJECT as well as accept; it is not a real provider and is
      registered nowhere.

      ⚠ NO Testcontainers AND NO Aspire.Hosting, AND THE HALF THAT NEEDS THEM IS NOT MISSING — IT IS
      IN ANOTHER PROJECT. docs/plan/03 § Providers lists five things every provider must pass, and two
      of them — "reconcile after a manual cluster mutation → drift corrected" against a real API
      server, and "kill the silo mid-create → resource still converges" — cannot be honestly claimed
      without a real cluster and real storage. All five now run in test/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance
      against a k3s container, a PostgreSQL container and a Redis container.

      They are NOT here, and a package reference is exactly what must not be added to close the gap: a
      Testcontainers reference in this .csproj would make THIS suite refuse to run without a Docker
      daemon, and this suite is the per-PR gate for every provider change. ClusterBackedConformanceTests
      is what is left behind — one visible skip naming where the other half lives, because this
      assembly cannot reference that one and would otherwise have no way to mention it.

      What the Docker-free half DOES prove, against Orleans.TestingHost with in-memory storage and an
      in-memory API server: the twelve-step write path end to end for a real provider, the operation
      lifecycle to Succeeded, read-back and api-version projection, tags, locks, delete, the
      cross-tenant 404, the 409 on a delete that races an operation, drift correction against the fake
      cluster, all four reconciler clauses, and — for the first time in this repository — ADR-013's
      seven mandatory labels asserted against output a real reconciler actually rendered.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ CA1000, "do not declare static members on generic types", is suppressed HERE ONLY and the
          reason is the design rather than convenience. The harness is generic on the provider case
          source precisely so that per-provider state is bound to a TYPE instead of to a mutable
          global — see ProviderTestCluster's remarks. Every static it declares is per-provider by
          construction, which is the thing CA1000 warns you will be confusing, and which is exactly
          what is wanted. The alternative the rule pushes toward is one mutable static shared by every
          provider's harness, plus a lock and an ordering rule.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1000</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.TestingHost"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Reminders"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../src/CyberCloud.ResourceManager/CyberCloud.ResourceManager.csproj"/>
        <!-- The real quota and index grains — steps 6 and 7 run against them, not against fakes. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../../src/CyberCloud.Tenancy/CyberCloud.Tenancy.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
